Yes, this smells like two related but distinct issues.

First thing I would check: your current “working” tree has an untracked file:

`angelslim/utils/dist_safety.py`

and tracked edits in:

`tools/train_eagle3_online.py`
`angelslim/utils/__init__.py`

That helper is exactly for the `torchrun --nproc_per_node=1` logging segfault path. If you port with git but do not commit/add untracked files, the new server may not actually have the fix. So first verify the remote copy has:

```bash
git status --short
rg -n "apply_single_rank_dist_safety|dist_safety" tools/train_eagle3_online.py angelslim/utils
```

The single-GPU behavior fits this perfectly:

- `python tools/train_eagle3_online.py` works because no distributed process group exists.
- `torchrun --nproc_per_node=1 ...` creates a distributed process group anyway.
- HF Trainer logging/end-of-train may call `all_gather` / `barrier`.
- On some CUDA/NCCL stacks, world-size-1 NCCL collectives segfault.
- `apply_single_rank_dist_safety()` short-circuits those no-op collectives.

For the multi-GPU crash, I would investigate in this order:

1. Confirm exact code parity.
   The working server and failing server should have the same commit plus the same dirty/untracked changes. Do not trust “git ported it” until `git diff` and `git status --short` match.

2. Run a tiny NCCL smoke test on the bad server.
   If basic `torchrun` `all_reduce` crashes, this is not AngelSlim. It is driver/CUDA/NCCL/PyTorch/IB/P2P.

3. Compare runtime versions between servers:
   `python -c "import torch, transformers, accelerate, deepspeed, flash_attn; print(torch.__version__, torch.version.cuda); print(transformers.__version__, accelerate.__version__, deepspeed.__version__)"`
   Also compare `nvidia-smi`.

4. Try NCCL isolation env vars:
   ```bash
   NCCL_DEBUG=INFO TORCH_DISTRIBUTED_DEBUG=DETAIL CUDA_LAUNCH_BLOCKING=1 \
   NCCL_IB_DISABLE=1 NCCL_P2P_DISABLE=1 \
   torchrun ...
   ```
   If this fixes it, suspect IB/RDMA or GPU peer access on that server.

5. Separate DeepSpeed from NCCL.
   Try ZeRO-2 or no DeepSpeed with a tiny model/short length if possible. If no-DS works but ZeRO-3 crashes, focus on DeepSpeed/NCCL/offload compatibility.

6. Separate flash-attn from training.
   The target wrapper defaults LLM target inference to `attn_implementation="flash_attention_2"`. A bad flash-attn build can segfault at first forward/backward-adjacent CUDA work. Try changing that temporarily to `"sdpa"` or `"eager"` in `target_model_wrapper.py`.

7. Rule out memory pressure.
   Multi-GPU online training loads the full frozen target on every rank, then computes target logits of shape roughly `batch x seq x vocab`. For Qwen3-4B at `2048 x 151936`, logits alone are large. Try `MODEL_MAX_LENGTH=512`, `training_time_test_length=1`, and maybe `PYTORCH_CUDA_ALLOC_CONF=expandable_segments:True`.

My strongest immediate bet: the one-GPU `torchrun` issue is because the untracked `dist_safety.py` patch did not travel. The multi-GPU issue is more likely environment/NCCL/DeepSpeed/flash-attn than the Python training logic, especially since model loading succeeds and plain Python works.
